From 459b22d71deeed349848c2b7652f932637b31da6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Pauli <paul.dale@oracle.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 14:27:54 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] doc: remove note about safe-math's muldiv being imperfect

There should be no inputs that error when they shouldn't.
The converse was and still is true.

Reviewed-by: Eugene Syromiatnikov <esyr@openssl.org>
Reviewed-by: Neil Horman <nhorman@openssl.org>
Merge-date: Tue Nov 24 18:13:19 2026
Merged-from: https://github.com/openssl/openssl/pull/33917
(cherry picked from commit f13c02fe2d47d063e568452a95c134aec4a2ebd9)
Upstream: https://github.com/openssl/openssl/commit/459b22d71deeed349848c2b7652f932637b31da6
---
 doc/internal/man3/OSSL_SAFE_MATH_SIGNED.pod | 8 --------
 1 file changed, 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/doc/internal/man3/OSSL_SAFE_MATH_SIGNED.pod b/doc/internal/man3/OSSL_SAFE_MATH_SIGNED.pod
index 6c78c3a..97371dc 100644
--- a/doc/internal/man3/OSSL_SAFE_MATH_SIGNED.pod
+++ b/doc/internal/man3/OSSL_SAFE_MATH_SIGNED.pod
@@ -61,14 +61,6 @@ safe_neg_TYPE() calculates the negation of I<a>.
 
 safe_abs_TYPE() calculates the absolute value of I<a>.
 
-=head1 NOTES
-
-The safe_muldiv_TYPE() function is not perfect.  There exist inputs where
-a valid result could be computed with infinite length integers but this
-function returns an error condition.  Such instances should, however,
-be rare in practice.  The converse is not true.  An invalid result will
-always be flagged as an error.
-
 =head1 RETURN VALUES
 
 All these functions return the result of the operation, if the operation
